  "Question": "In a right triangle with sides \\(a\\) and \\(b,\\) and hypotenuse \\(c,\\) the altitude drawn on the hypotenuse is \\(x.\\) Then:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(ab=x^2\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac1a+\\dfrac1b=\\dfrac1x\\)",
    "C": "\\(a^2+b^2=2x^2\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\dfrac1{x^2}=\\dfrac1{a^2}+\\dfrac1{b^2}\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\dfrac1x=\\dfrac ba\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "The triangle's area connects the altitude \\(x\\) to the three side lengths. Using the perpendicular legs as base and height gives area \\(ab/2\\); using the hypotenuse and its altitude gives area \\(cx/2\\). Thus\n\\[\n\\frac{ab}{2}=\\frac{cx}{2},\\qquad x=\\frac{ab}{c}.\n\\]\nThe Pythagorean theorem gives \\(c^2=a^2+b^2\\). All side lengths and the altitude are positive, so taking reciprocals and simplifying is valid:\n\\[\n\\frac1{x^2}\n=\\frac{c^2}{a^2b^2}\n=\\frac{a^2+b^2}{a^2b^2}\n=\\frac1{b^2}+\\frac1{a^2}.\n\\]\nTherefore the correct identity is choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{D}}\\).",
